**How the licence conclusions were made.** These are conclusions about the grants located, not guarantees that every app screenshot is free of third-party rights. Dataset licences, code licences, pretrained-weight licences, and the legal treatment of trained weights are separate questions. None of the inspected permissive licences requires publishing fine-tuned weights merely because the dataset was used; none resolves a separate base-model licence. For model redistribution, retain a source/attribution manifest, licence links and modification notices where applicable, and avoid embedding raw dataset examples in the release without checking the relevant data terms.

| Grant | Relevant licence text / consequence |
|---|---|
| CC BY 4.0 | Section 2 permits “reproduce and Share” and “produce, reproduce, and Share Adapted Material”; section 3 imposes attribution and modification notices. There is no noncommercial restriction. It supports commercial training and distribution to the extent the licensor controls the relevant rights; it is not a guarantee about unrelated screenshot copyrights. [Legal text](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode.en) |
| Apache 2.0 | Section 2 grants rights to “reproduce, prepare Derivative Works of, publicly display, publicly perform, sublicense, and distribute”. Section 4 supplies notice/licence requirements. A dataset card declaring Apache is evidence of the publisher's intended grant, not a power to relicense somebody else's source images. [Legal text](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0) |
| MIT code | Microsoft's GUI-Actor licence applies to the “Software and associated documentation files”. That is not an express licence to every third-party dataset linked by the project. [Actual licence](https://github.com/microsoft/GUI-Actor/blob/main/LICENSE) |
| Rico media | The original terms say screenshots “may contain copyrighted work”; clause 3 conditions sharing with research colleagues on accepting the terms, and clause 4 binds a commercial employer. This is **not an explicit noncommercial ban**, but it also is not an unconditional CC BY grant for all screenshot content or model redistribution. [Original copyright terms](https://www.interactionmining.org/archive/rico/copyright.txt) |
| Apple Ferret-UI | The official README labels the data CC BY-NC 4.0 and restricts use to research, including models using the dataset. Commercial deployment is not supported by that grant. [Official README](https://github.com/apple/ml-ferret/tree/main/ferretui), [CC BY-NC legal text](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode.en) |

**Split and AndroidWorld checks.** I compared recoverable episode IDs against local `data/meta/splits.json` and the [official Google split JSON](https://storage.googleapis.com/gresearch/android_control/splits.json). They agree: 13,603 train, 137 validation and 1,543 test episodes. The following results concern source-episode provenance, not merely an HF split named `train`.

| AndroidControl derivative | Full annotation audit | AC official test / validation overlap | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|---|
| GUI-Actor `androidcontrol_bbox.json` | 39,701 rows; 12,419 unique episode IDs recovered from `android_control_episode_[ID]_STEP.png` | **0 test; 0 validation; 0 unknown** | Strong evidence that this particular file uses official train only. Does not establish an AW-app filter. [Audit](datasets_scratch/actor_audit.json) |
| GUI-Libra `android_control_reasoning_and_grounding_changecoord_1000.json` | 14,664 rows; 5,676 episode IDs from `ID/screenshot_STEP.png` | **0 test; 0 validation; 0 unknown** | Same conclusion for this file, not every dataset in the 81K mixture. [Audit](datasets_scratch/libra_audit.json) |
| UI-S1 `train_1000.jsonl` | 1,000 episodes, 6,536 steps; screenshots reindexed as `0_0`, etc. | Direct source-ID intersection **cannot be performed** | 10 rows have exact normalized goal matches to 9 official test episodes: 12289, 5215, 11714, 11448, 5704, 15785, 3484, 1569, 4988. This is a contamination warning, **not proof of identical trajectories**. Reject until original IDs or image joins establish provenance. [Audit with row numbers](datasets_scratch/uis1_audit.json) |

For other sources, no AndroidControl ancestry was identified except where stated. Their independent provenance reduces AC-test risk, but a full screenshot-hash intersection against the frozen test was not performed under this download budget. Numeric episode IDs in unrelated datasets are not comparable to AC IDs.

I used the packages in [AndroidWorld's official setup code](https://github.com/google-research/android_world/blob/main/android_world/env/setup_device/apps.py), rather than matching generic app names such as “Tasks” or “Audio Recorder”:

```text
Simple Calendar Pro   com.simplemobiletools.calendar.pro
Markor                net.gsantner.markor
Pro Expense           com.arduia.expense
Broccoli              com.flauschcode.broccoli
Joplin                net.cozic.joplin
OsmAnd                net.osmand
Retro Music           code.name.monkey.retromusic
VLC                   org.videolan.vlc
Simple SMS Messenger  com.simplemobiletools.smsmessenger
Tasks                 org.tasks
Audio Recorder        com.dimowner.audiorecorder
Simple Draw Pro       com.simplemobiletools.draw.pro
OpenTracks            de.dennisguse.opentracks
Simple Gallery Pro    com.simplemobiletools.gallery.pro
```

The conservative family filter additionally includes `net.osmand.plus` and `org.videolan.vlc.betav7neon`. An old Rico screenshot of VLC is app-domain overlap, **not evidence it was collected using AndroidWorld or its task specifications**. Conversely, a rollout generated inside AW can compromise a strict held-out-environment claim even if its exact instruction differs from one of the 116 benchmark tasks.

The [original Rico `ui_details.csv`](https://storage.googleapis.com/crowdstf-rico-uiuc-4540/rico_dataset_v0.1/ui_details.csv) maps screen IDs to packages. Full small-file joins found:

| Source actually scanned | Rows / images scanned | Exact-14-package overlap | With conservative VLC-beta family alias |
|---|---|---|---|
| ScreenQA train, each answer representation | 68,951 QA rows / 28,378 images | 28 QA rows | **48 rows / 23 images** |
| Atlas UIBert | 5,682 images / 16,660 element references | 3 images / 9 references | **Same** |
| Atlas Widget Captioning | 101,426 reference rows / 14,409 images | 50 rows | **87 rows / 10 images** |
| Atlas RICOSCA | 173,275 reference rows / 18,148 images | 102 rows | **149 rows / 18 images** |
| Screen2Words, all splits | 112,085 captions / 22,417 images | 90 captions | **105 captions / 21 images; 80 captions in train** |
| Original Widget Captioning, public Rico part | 48,325 widget rows / 17,435 images | 19 widget rows | **34 widget rows / 10 images; all 34 are in train** |
| ComplexQA | 11,781 questions | 11 questions | **15 questions / 13 images** |
| MoIT downloaded train portions: find-icon / widget-caption / summary | 15,135 / 15,580 / 8,000 rows | 14 / 6 / 5 rows | **20 / 11 / 7 rows**, respectively |

These are counted overlaps, not prevalence estimates for unscanned files. The final exact-package counts are in [the corrected Rico audit](datasets_scratch/rico_aw_exact_audit.json) and [additional joins](datasets_scratch/additional_rico_audit.json). The earlier broad-name diagnostic `rico_audit.json` includes unrelated recorder apps and must **not** be used as the AW contamination count.

**1. GUI-Odyssey — viable new trajectories, with a real conversion cost.** The current release is [HF `hflqf88888/GUIOdyssey`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/hflqf88888/GUIOdyssey/tree/main). Its complete `all_annot.json` index is 2,862,103 bytes and contains **8,334 episodes / 127,893 indexed steps**. The older [OpenGVLab repository card](https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenGVLab/GUI-Odyssey) describes **7,735 episodes, 201 apps and 118,791 screenshots** and points to the new release; these are versions of a dataset, not two independent corpora. The newer paper reports 212 apps; raw app-label aliases are not a reliable count of distinct packages. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08451), [local index audit](datasets_scratch/odyssey_audit.json).

Images are a split ZIP, `screenshots/screenshots.z01` through `.z08` plus `screenshots.zip`, **92,639,510,437 bytes** in total. Annotation JSONs are separately accessible. A ranged sample yielded a real 2208×1840 PNG. Indexed device dimensions include 720×1280, 1080×2400, 1344×2992, 1440×3120, 2560×1600 and both orientations of 2208×1840. Full archive CRC/completeness remains unverified. Raw download works; do not assume `load_dataset()` works from the card's configuration. The downloaded [`splits/random_split.json`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/hflqf88888/GUIOdyssey/blob/main/splits/random_split.json) separates **6,668 train episodes / 102,086 steps** and **1,666 test episodes / 25,807 steps**; use its train IDs for the proposed quota. Other app/task/device splits exist; they are alternate partitions, not extra data. The device split holds out 1,381 tablet episodes. [Split/index join](datasets_scratch/odyssey_split_audit.json).

Three complete episode files were inspected. Actions include `CLICK`, `LONG_PRESS`, `TEXT`, `SCROLL`, `COMPLETE`, `INCOMPLETE`, and special-key click payloads such as `KEY_HOME`, `KEY_BACK`, `KEY_APPSELECT`. The actual sample uses **`TEXT`**, so an adapter accepting only a README's `TYPE` spelling would lose examples. Points are normalized 0–1000; scrolls contain start/end points and sometimes a gesture path. Point/text/back/home map naturally. Recents/app-selector has no matching action. General gestures, container selection and travel distance cannot be represented losslessly by the project's direction-only scroll. An incomplete attempt is not automatically proof that the user's task is infeasible. There are no substantive screen-QA answer labels in the inspected trajectory schema.

Collection used its own Android emulators; downloaded [episode metadata](datasets_scratch/odyssey_example3.json) records Android 14/API 34, rather than the AW emulator configuration. The full index has app **names**, not package IDs. No exact names of the 14 targets were found, but **8 episodes** mention `Simple Calendar` or `Simple Calendar - easy planner`; quarantine these until their packages are identified. The generic `Calendar` label appears in 421 episodes and needs package resolution or conservative exclusion too; it cannot certify an AW-clean calendar source. Home-screen icons and intermediate unlisted apps are beyond this index check.

Human demonstrations are stronger evidence than the subsequently generated GPT-4o instructions, context and SAM2 boxes. Do not use post-hoc context as causal history: an inspected record describes text already entered before the corresponding `TEXT` action. [Issue 19](https://github.com/OpenGVLab/GUI-Odyssey/issues/19) reports 780/25,807 checked cases with dimension-order differences; the author explains that device metadata can differ from rotated screenshots and that normalized coordinates use actual screenshots. That is **not** a demonstrated 3% coordinate-error rate. Older [missing-image reports](https://github.com/OpenGVLab/GUI-Odyssey/issues/16) justify a manifest/CRC check but should not be attributed automatically to the new archive.

The dataset card explicitly declares CC BY 4.0. **USE-WITH-FILTER**: new version only; reconcile actual image orientation; exclude unresolved AW-app labels; retain only supported actions/gestures; review incomplete labels; reconstruct previous actions causally; keep original touch points instead of replacing them blindly with generated box centres. Prefer phone-resolution tasks and short visible-state decisions for 450M models. Cross-app transfer is promising for AW, but tasks requiring memory beyond the current screenshot and last eight text-history entries need rejection or explicit history availability. Expected effort **12–20 h**, more if selective remote-ZIP acquisition is built. Full download at 100 Mbps has an ideal lower bound of about **2.1 h**, excluding overhead.

**2. AMEX — prioritize its 38,709 actual action rows, not its headline element count.** [HF `Yuxiang007/AMEX`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Yuxiang007/AMEX/tree/main) is public. `AMEX/instruction_anno.zip` is **3,105,724 bytes** and was downloaded in full. It contains **3,046 episodes / 38,709 steps**. Its eight image parts plus final ZIP total **93,481,307,584 bytes**; `element_anno.zip` is another **115,510,591 bytes**. The publisher's ~104K screenshots and 110 apps include additional autonomous screen exploration, not 104K demonstrated agent decisions. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2407.17490), [complete annotation audit](datasets_scratch/amex_audit.json).

| AMEX released action | Count | Conversion |
|---|---:|---|
| TAP | 24,815 | `click`, pixel `[x,y]` normalized using actual image dimensions |
| SWIPE | 7,628 | Only validated cardinal page scrolling fits; endpoints/distance are lost |
| TYPE | 2,419 | `input_text`; preserve its separation from Enter |
| TASK_COMPLETE | 2,828 | `status complete` on the supplied observation |
| TASK_IMPOSSIBLE | 220 | `status infeasible` only after reviewing the failure reason/context |
| PRESS_ENTER | 651 | `keyboard_enter` — useful coverage missing from many corpora |
| PRESS_BACK / PRESS_HOME | 135 / 13 | Direct mappings |

No long-press action occurs in this released trajectory annotation set. The schema includes `instruction`, `episode_id`, `steps`, `touch_coord`, `lift_coord`, `device_dim`, `package_name`, `type_text`, `need_human_check`, `interest_region`, and `image_path`. Resolutions span 1080×2280, 1080×2400, 1440×2960/3040/3120 and a small landscape tail; a real sampled PNG is 1440×2960. The paper describes AVD/Genymotion collection with Appium, not AW task collection. Scanning **all 38,709 step package fields found zero exact matches to the 14 AW packages**. This finding covers trajectories, not every autonomous screenshot or home-screen icon. `com.google.android.apps.tasks` is not AW's `org.tasks`.

There are **77 `need_human_check` steps in 56 episodes**. Two episodes contain duplicate/conflicting terminal actions: `f284f97becb6477d9aa9731295908d18` has two impossible markers; `63eeb12752a6426abeb129b8049d5bdd` has impossible followed by complete. Exclude these episodes unless manually repaired. The paper reports over 97% accuracy for functionality descriptions after its verification process; this is not a measured error rate for all action labels. It reports sizeable AndroidControl gains in its own 7B training experiments, with smaller gains under equal-row replacement; do not transfer those gains numerically to a 450M–2B model.

The released functionality coverage is materially smaller than a naive reading of the headline ~711K element count. In [the author's issue response](https://github.com/yuxiangchai/AMEX-codebase/issues/2), the author confirms roughly **300K functionality descriptions on ~20K screens** and says this is the full version. Many elements have empty descriptions. Code availability and its licence do not change the data's **CC BY 4.0** card declaration.

**USE-WITH-FILTER**: exclude flagged/inconsistent episodes, preserve separate Enter, filter gesture meaning, reject invalid/missing screenshots and unresolved impossible cases, then balance apps and actions. Stronger incremental value than another large pile of synthetic boxes, particularly for typing/Enter/terminal behavior. Estimated **10–18 h**; full media transfer about **2.1 h at an ideal 100 Mbps**. Reuse this image cache if testing Aria or Atlas AMEX labels.

**3. GUI-Actor AndroidControl bbox — clean official-train IDs, weak incremental acquisition value.** The exact files are [`cckevinn/GUI-Actor-Data/androidcontrol_bbox.json`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/cckevinn/GUI-Actor-Data/blob/main/androidcontrol_bbox.json), **25,035,159 bytes**, and `AndroidControl_images.zip`, **49,306,113,518 bytes**. The full JSON contains **39,701 unique image rows**, not the paper's rounded 47K description. All recoverable IDs are official train, as shown above. Existing local AC screenshots can supply these same images, so downloading the ZIP is unnecessary.

Each row contains an image path, a human referring instruction, an assistant `pyautogui.click(x=..., y=...)` response with unit-interval coordinates, and normalized `bbox_gt` XYXY. All 39,701 responses use click. There are no trajectory histories, scrolls, terminals or answers. Converting a point to the 0–1000 grid is straightforward; converting an entire box into one point discards the box supervision. A normal point-output fine-tune also does not reproduce GUI-Actor's coordinate-free attention architecture. I found no invalid normalized boxes in the scan, but **400 boxes cover more than half the screen**; inspect them rather than treating large regions as precise targets. [Project and training configuration](https://github.com/microsoft/GUI-Actor), [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03143).

The HF card has no explicit data licence or licence file. The linked Microsoft code is MIT; original AC terms are conflicting. The JSON has no app/package fields, so apply the project's existing AW exclusion **after joining back to source episodes**, not merely the train-ID allowlist. **REJECT as an additional v2 source**: it adds neither new screenshots nor full-action supervision, and commercial annotation rights are unresolved. If grounding ablation is needed, reweight original AC low-level click rows first. A later licensed, filtered Actor alternative costs **3–6 h** and no new image download; it should replace, not duplicate, those rows.

**4. OS-Atlas non-AW mobile — usable components, not a clean monolithic source.** The [actual `mobile_domain/` listing](https://huggingface.co/datasets/OS-Copilot/OS-Atlas-data/tree/main/mobile_domain) contains:

| File(s) | Bytes / contents verified |
|---|---|
| `amex_images_part_aa`, `_ab`, `_ac` | 92,099,634,755 bytes combined; AMEX repackaging |
| `amex_raw.json` | 327,588,775 bytes; only a bounded prefix was inspected |
| `UIBert.zip`, `uibert_raw.json` | 525,599,155 + 5,296,135 bytes; full JSON: 5,682 screens / 16,660 references |
| `rico_imgs.zip` | 6,513,082,985 bytes; shared Rico image pool |
| `widget_captioning.json` | 13,308,570 bytes; full JSON: 101,426 references / 14,409 screens |
| `ricosca.json` | 28,499,008 bytes; full JSON: 173,275 references / 18,148 screens |
| **`aw_mobile.json`, `mobile_images.zip`** | **31,860,297 + 2,325,202,926 bytes; exclude both**. Full JSON: 15,905 screens / 89,860 element references |

The [publisher README](https://huggingface.co/datasets/OS-Copilot/OS-Atlas-data/blob/main/README.md) explicitly identifies its AndroidWorld source. The other components predate or are independent of AW collection, but the package joins above prove residual VLC/OsmAnd overlap. UIBert sample imagery is 1080×1920; Rico is mixed original Android dimensions and AMEX retains its higher-resolution source family. Do not assume one fixed resolution from an archive's name.

These are grounding records, using normalized XYXY boxes and referring text, with either nested image/element records or flat references. They have **no demonstrated agent action sequence or terminal/answer semantics**. Reference text can describe a noninteractive label or an operation such as swiping, so turning every box into a click is not lossless. UIBert RefExp is preferable to a large templated RICOSCA expansion; repeated Widget Captioning expressions are not independent screenshots. Original [UIBert](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/uibert) explicitly licenses its data CC BY 4.0; the Atlas Apache card cannot remove that attribution or the original Rico terms.

**USE-WITH-FILTER** as a small grounding alternative: remove both AW files, join Rico IDs to packages, enforce source train splits where recoverable, exclude noninteractive/ambiguous targets, select one paraphrase per image/box, and deduplicate against AMEX/Aria/original Widget Captioning. No blanket lossless-conversion or commercially cleared-all-sources claim is justified. Budget **6–12 h**; Rico/UIBert-only media is **7.04 GB**, around nine minutes at ideal 100 Mbps, versus ~2.2 h for all non-AW mobile media/labels. Default mix allocation is zero until a grounding deficit is measured.

**5. Aria-UI mobile — a synthetic label expansion of AMEX.** The file is [`Aria-UI/Aria-UI_Data/mobile/aria_ui_mobile_AMEX_1077k.json`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Aria-UI/Aria-UI_Data/tree/main/mobile), **1,632,415,539 bytes**. The publisher describes ~104K AMEX screens and ~1.1M instructions; the filename suggests 1,077K. I inspected a 1 MB prefix containing 47 complete image records, **not all million instructions**. The mobile folder does not provide a new mobile screenshot archive: retrieve the corresponding AMEX images, potentially another 93.48 GB if uncached.

Records contain `image_path`, `clickable_elements`, pixel `bbox`, 0–1000 `bbox_norm`, XML descriptions, functionality text, long/short element captions and multiple instructions. This is grounding supervision, not multi-step demonstrations. A first sampled MyObservatory element has XML text “Load URL” while generated text interprets it as browser forward history: a concrete **semantic disagreement**, not a visually verified error rate. Other generated requests involve copying or entering URLs and cannot automatically become a one-click action. Select only unambiguous, visible, genuinely actionable references and one short phrasing per target.

The Apache-labelled new annotations retain AMEX image obligations. No AW package fields are present in the inspected Aria records; join back to original AMEX metadata, and do not extend the trajectory-only zero-overlap result to all 104K screens. The [paper's 44.8% AW result](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.16256) uses a GPT-4o planner and an Aria grounder with history; it is not the performance of a small standalone screenshot-only policy. The card's warnings about broken older desktop image mappings concern desktop files, not a demonstrated defect in this mobile file.

**USE-WITH-FILTER** for a tightly bounded grounding ablation, not a million-row addition. Estimated **6–12 h** once AMEX is cached; annotation-only transfer roughly two minutes at ideal 100 Mbps. Default allocation **zero** because the recommended mix already uses AMEX and because original AC provides grounding supervision. If selected, replace 2–4K rows, rather than counting AMEX, Atlas-AMEX and Aria-AMEX as three diverse sources.

**6. Google Rico-derived data — the answer source is real, but the media and task units matter.** The original screenshots/view hierarchies are hosted at [Rico `unique_uis.tar.gz`](https://storage.googleapis.com/crowdstf-rico-uiuc-4540/rico_dataset_v0.1/unique_uis.tar.gz), **6,471,262,799 bytes**. A ranged gzip sample contained a real 1080×1920 JPEG. The [official Rico download page](https://www.interactionmining.org/archive/rico) supplies the metadata and original terms. Annotations under CC BY do not convert all this media to CC BY.

**ScreenQA.** The real host is [`google-research-datasets/screen_qa`](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/screen_qa), with `answers_and_bboxes/{train,validation,test}.json` and `short_answers/{train,validation,test}.json`. Full train labels are **20,193,256 bytes**; short train labels **8,681,944 bytes**. The release has 85,984 question instances on 35,352 screens: **68,951 / 8,614 / 8,419** questions in train/validation/test. Both answer representations describe the same questions, so they must not be counted as independent samples. The full train metadata has 53,388 rows at 1080×1920, 15,560 at 540×960 and three at 1920×1080; actual image dimensions govern any geometry conversion.

Full rows contain `image_id`, width/height, question, and `ground_truth` with `full_answer` plus UI text and pixel bounds. Short answers are generated variants. A question becomes the goal, history is `None`, and a selected grounded answer becomes `{"action_type":"answer","text":"..."}`. This is a good semantic fit for single-screen answering; there is no navigation history or evidence of task completion to synthesize separately. Do not turn answer-text boxes into clicks. Human full answers have stronger provenance than model-generated shortenings; train has fewer independent raters than evaluation. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2209.08199), [explicit dataset licence](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/screen_qa/blob/main/LICENSE).

The [black-image issue](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/screen_qa/issues/2) reports screen 37374 with a meaningful Ford-related label but a black image. This is a credible mismatch report, not proof of a corpus-wide error rate. **USE-WITH-FILTER**: original train only, remove the 48 conservative AW-app rows, inspect image/label alignment, exclude answers unreadable after the actual model resize, and choose one short or full answer per question. Estimated **4–8 h** and the shared 6.47 GB media download. Its main value is filling the `answer` behavior gap, not increasing next-click accuracy. Use brief answers especially for 450M.

**ComplexQA.** The same official repository's `complex_qa/data.json` is **1,290,719 bytes / 11,781 questions**. It is primarily a reasoning evaluation resource with counting and relational questions, not a ready-made official train split. The complete join finds 15 conservative AW-app questions. **REJECT from the default training mix** to preserve its evaluation value and avoid pretending it has a publisher train split. A later explicitly defined research-training partition would need screen-level separation from every other Rico evaluation source, visual validation of generated answers, and the same media/licence conditions. Estimated 4–8 h additional; no new image download. Expected value is small-screen reasoning, with greater noise and legibility risk at 450M.

**Screen2Words.** The real host is [`google-research-datasets/screen2words`](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/screen2words), not an assumed HF mirror. `screen_summaries.csv` is **5,313,544 bytes / 112,085 rows**, five human summaries per **22,417** Rico screens. `split/` defines **15,743 train screens / 78,715 train captions**, plus dev/test. Select at most one summary per screen if ever used. The original README/tree contains no explicit licence file or dataset grant located in this audit; a downstream CC BY tag and Google's code-repository Apache licence do not settle it. **REJECT for current v2**, both for unresolved terms and low marginal value compared with direct ScreenQA. Conversion to a broad “describe this screen” answer is possible, but it teaches neither navigation nor precise task-state answers. It shares old Rico media, has the quantified AW-app overlap above, and needs the same image/readability checks. Estimate **3–6 h** if terms are resolved.

**Widget Captioning.** The real host is [`google-research-datasets/widget-caption`](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/widget-caption), specifically `widget_captions.csv` and the split files. The CSV is **5,007,978 bytes**. Parsing finds **61,285 rows** versus the README's 61,286 headline: **48,325 `rc` rows** for public Rico widgets and **12,960 `nr` rows whose privately collected screenshots are not released**. The public Rico portion spans 17,435 screens, with **41,221 train widget rows**. Counting pipe-separated caption alternatives as independent widgets would inflate scale.

`nodeId` is a dotted view-hierarchy path, not a coordinate. The README gives the exact traversal from `activity.root` through `children`; join the original Rico JSON to recover the target rectangle, validate alignment, and then normalize. Captions describe purpose and may describe a multi-step effect. Either construct a well-specified region-question answer, or use only instructions that truly identify a clickable target; arbitrary caption-to-click conversion is lossy. No trajectory, completion or infeasibility labels exist. Explicit **CC BY 4.0 annotations** coexist with Rico terms. **USE-WITH-FILTER**: `rc` + original train only, package filter, valid visible node, one paraphrase, verified target semantics. At **8–12 h** from original files, it is a secondary option; Atlas preprocessing lowers adapter cost but requires split provenance checks and deduplication. Default allocation zero.

**Additional corporate-source search and credible releases.** I searched the named companies' primary project pages, GitHub repositories, HF cards/file APIs and relevant papers. A paper result, a model checkpoint, a code licence, or a dataset named `META-GUI` is not itself a downloadable training-data release from the company. “Not found” below is bounded by those searches, not a proof that no public release exists anywhere.

| Company | Verified result and disposition |
|---|---|
| Microsoft | GUI-Actor above; **GUI-Libra** below; Microsoft-associated **ShowUI** releases desktop/web grounding data, while its mobile training instructions reuse AMEX, GUIAct and AitW. Do not count a separate new ShowUI mobile corpus. [Official training instructions](https://github.com/showlab/ShowUI/blob/main/TRAIN.md), [Microsoft GUI-Libra publication](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/gui-libra-data-efficient-post-training-for-reliable-reasoning-and-acting-in-native-gui-agents/) |
| Meta | No qualifying new downloadable mobile-agent/grounding training corpus verified in the searched primary channels, including the [Facebook HF dataset catalogue](https://huggingface.co/api/datasets?author=facebook&limit=1000). **META-GUI is an academic task-oriented dialogue benchmark, not a Meta corporate release**. [Actual authors/project](https://x-lance.github.io/META-GUI-Leaderboard/) |
| Google | AC, AitW, Rico derivatives and UIBert are real. **Screen Annotation** and **PixelHelp/seq2act** are public additional resources, assessed below. ScreenAI's entire large synthetic training mixture is not equivalent to the small released evaluation datasets. [ScreenAI paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04615) |
| Apple | **Ferret-UI** publishes tiny sample JSONs and model assets; the inspected repository did not expose its claimed complete training corpus. Data terms are research-only/CC BY-NC. Later Ferret-UI papers or model releases do not establish a new complete data release. **REJECT** for this commercial-capable acquisition plan. [Official repository](https://github.com/apple/ml-ferret/tree/main/ferretui), [Ferret-UI Lite research page](https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/ferret-ui) |
| Amazon | **R-VLM** is a model/research release using existing GUI data, not a verified new Amazon mobile screenshot corpus. Its paper uses the existing SeeClick mixture and GUI evaluations; region/zoom advantages are not directly transferable to the one-image token budget. No qualifying new corpus was verified. [Amazon publication](https://www.amazon.science/publications/r-vlm-region-aware-vision-language-model-for-precise-gui-grounding) |
| Samsung | **MoIT/MoUI**, jointly with IIT Bombay, has real public training JSONs. It was not reasonable to dismiss it as unavailable. Licence is unresolved; detailed audit below. [Official repository](https://github.com/iitbsrib/MoUI), [paper with Samsung affiliations](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1295/) |
| NVIDIA | **LocateAnything-Data** includes OS-Atlas and other GUI sources, but is primarily a repackaging for grounding. Detailed rejection below. [Official release](https://huggingface.co/datasets/NVEagle/LocateAnything-Data) |
| Alibaba | **CMGUI** is a real March 2026 release; **UI-S1** is a real AC-derived release; Aria-UI includes Alibaba-associated work. GUI-Owl or other model checkpoints are not proof that their complete training corpora are downloadable. [CMGUI](https://huggingface.co/datasets/alibabagroup/CMGUI), [UI-S1](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/tree/main/UI-S1) |
| ByteDance | **UI-TARS** publishes an example training JSON and evaluation/example files, not the complete training corpus in the inspected tree. **GUI-ReWalk** has code but no verified bulk dataset download; its dataset-release issue asks for that missing release. **REJECT acquisition claims**, rather than inventing a downloadable size/count. [UI-TARS tree](https://github.com/bytedance/UI-TARS/tree/main/data), [GUI-ReWalk](https://github.com/bytedance/GUI-ReWalk), [release issue](https://github.com/bytedance/GUI-ReWalk/issues/3) |
| Tencent | **AppAgent/AppAgentX** supply frameworks and evaluation tasks, not a verified new public training-trajectory collection. **OSWorkerBench** is desktop/browser work, not Android. **REJECT platform/availability mismatch**. [AppAgent test set](https://github.com/TencentQQGYLab/AppAgent/blob/main/assets/testset.md), [OSWorkerBench](https://huggingface.co/datasets/tencent/OSWorkerBench) |

**GUI-Libra — useful curation, not 81K independent new demonstrations.** [HF `GUI-Libra/GUI-Libra-81K-SFT`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/GUI-Libra/GUI-Libra-81K-SFT/tree/main) contains multiple coordinate/reasoning variants of the same examples and source-specific image tar parts. Publisher scale is ~81K steps / ~9K trajectories, with a web component; **do not sum four annotation variants**. Image archives total about 161.54 GB: AC 35.61 GB, AMEX 25.67 GB, Odyssey 83.97 GB, plus AitW, Android-in-the-Zoo and web sources. The full downloaded AC annotation variant is 63,039,097 bytes and yields the clean official-train intersection above. Reuse originals instead of its AC media archive.

The paper describes action re-prediction and grounding consistency filtering, which can make its labels useful. Sample outputs use long `<thinking>`/`<answer>` wrappers and an action space including point-conditioned `Write`, `Swipe`, seconds-valued `Wait`, and `Terminate`. These are not drop-in labels for short `<think>` plus one project JSON action: combined focus-and-write, raw swipes and wait duration lose semantics; only current screenshots are permitted here. Prefer the short/no-reasoning variant or produce a short causal intent, and preserve original source provenance through the rewrite. [Official project](https://github.com/GUI-Libra/GUI-Libra).

**USE-WITH-FILTER** as an alternative selection of AC/AMEX/Odyssey, not an additive multiplier. Audit every selected source separately; Android-in-the-Zoo does not inherit clearance simply because this mixture says Apache. AW-package joins remain necessary for the AC subset. Default allocation zero because direct sources provide simpler provenance. Expected cost **6–12 h per selected source**, and no distinct measured benefit for these small models has been established.

**CMGUI — a real Alibaba source, but not needed by the default English mix.** The complete [HF train annotation file](https://huggingface.co/datasets/alibabagroup/CMGUI/blob/main/episode_annotation_train.jsonl), 27,640,096 bytes, contains **18,560 episodes / 98,168 steps**. Test has **386 / 2,547**. Train images are `screenshot_zip/screenshot_train.z01`–`.z33` plus `.zip`: **170,739,775,128 bytes**; test images add 4,499,514,184 bytes and should not be acquired for training. These are multi-volume ZIP parts, not independently selectable image shards. [Primary card and licence](https://huggingface.co/datasets/alibabagroup/CMGUI/blob/main/README.md), [Alibaba project](https://github.com/alibaba/MobiZen-GUI).

Actual train actions: **76,333 CLICK, 9,873 TYPE, 7,509 STOP, 4,310 SWIPE, 43 LONG_PRESS, 31 KEY_BACK, 54 KEY_HOME, 15 WAIT**. Pixels and screenshot dimensions are explicit. Observed dimensions are mostly 1440×3200 and 1080×2400, with 720×1560/1280 and landscape variants. Click, long-press, text, back/home, wait and genuine STOP can be mapped; swipes have the same loss as AMEX. There is no demonstrated answer/infeasible category. **11,051 episodes lack STOP**, so the last interaction is not a labelled completed-state screenshot.

No exact AW-app name appears in the train app-name field; however, `日历` (Calendar), `笔记` (Notes), `Recorder` and generic system names are not package identification. Exclude unresolved generic apps for a strict app-held-out claim. No AC origin is declared, so its local numeric IDs must not be intersected directly with AC IDs. Both CMGUI splits restart episode IDs at zero: the meaningful check instead found **2,148 shared screenshot filenames**, affecting **332 of 386 test episodes**. This proves shared media references, not necessarily identical full trajectories; it makes CMGUI's own nominal train/test separation unsafe without additional filtering. Remove any train episode touching a CMGUI-test filename if retaining that benchmark. [Full audit](datasets_scratch/cmgui_audit.json).

The publisher claims human verification and precise boxes, but no independent corpus-wide error rate was established. **USE-WITH-FILTER** for Chinese deployment: source split/media separation, generic-app exclusion, supported gestures, and no fabricated STOP. CC BY 4.0 is explicit and different from the framework's Apache licence. Estimate **8–16 h**, plus **3.8 h ideal transfer at 100 Mbps**. Its Chinese OCR and app semantics compete with scarce English training capacity; allocate zero by default, or replace 2–4K trajectory rows in a multilingual ablation.

**UI-S1 — reject until source provenance is restored.** [HF `mPLUG/UI_S1_dataset`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/mPLUG/UI_S1_dataset/tree/main) supplies `train_1000.jsonl` (**1,015,926 bytes**) and `images.parquet` (**3,470,098,427 bytes**). The full JSONL has **1,000 trajectories / 6,536 steps**, including 3,380 clicks, 782 swipes, 391 types, 376 opens, 372 waits, 227 system buttons, eight long presses and 1,000 successful terminations. The [official UI-S1 README](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/tree/main/UI-S1) identifies AndroidControl as its source.

Source pixels/bboxes and action histories can mostly be adapted subject to the same swipe/focus checks, but screenshot dimensions must be read from the Parquet media; this audit did not download that payload. Renumbered image keys prevent an official-episode allowlist. The 10 matching test goals are enough to reject a blanket “train-only, no leakage” claim, although not enough to prove screenshot reuse. **REJECT** for this evaluation setup. Recovery would require original-ID mapping or pixel hashes against official AC train/test before any selection. It adds little diversity to the existing source, and neither its packaging nor its public download resolves AC's licence conflict.

**GUIAct smartphone — a cheap fallback that must not be sold as QA supervision.** [HF `yiye2023/GUIAct`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/yiye2023/GUIAct/tree/main) hosts `smartphone_train_data.json` (**44,287,601 bytes**) and `smartphone_train_images.parquet` (**4,267,662,999 bytes**). Full annotation parsing gives **65,627 rows / 7,246 episodes**: 38,262 taps, 11,670 swipes, 6,560 inputs, **1,889 Enter actions**, and **7,246 terminal “answers”**. Specifically, 7,236 say `task complete` and 10 say `task impossible`; none supplies a factual screen answer. This corrects “all complete” without changing the conclusion about missing QA. [Audit](datasets_scratch/guiact_audit.json), [official GUICourse repository](https://github.com/RUCBM/GUICourse).

Its AitW-General-derived smartphone records include goals, history, pixel and normalized points, and image dimensions: 412×732, 540×1080/1140/1170 and 720×1440/1480/1520. The [paper's action-conversion appendix](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.11317) confirms that Back/Home were converted into taps on the navigation bar; their original action identity is therefore absent. A tap/Enter/type/terminal adapter is simple, but navigation-bar taps require the expected visible buttons, and raw swipes still lose target/distance semantics. Reconstruct causal history rather than blindly trusting formatted histories. No app/package field in these annotation rows permits a comprehensive AW-package count; recover it through AitW episode IDs or exclude unresolved examples. It was not collected as AW rollouts and is not AC-derived. Low resolution and AitW's repeated task templates constrain expected gain, while low download cost and Enter coverage are useful.

The official project explicitly declares CC BY 4.0 for datasets; the HF metadata says Apache. Both are permissive but differ in obligations, so retain attribution rather than treating the HF tag as licence erasure. The original AitW-specific data grant was not established in this audit, so commercial clearance of the repackaged source also remains unresolved. **USE-WITH-FILTER** as an 8K-row research replacement for AMEX when transfer/storage costs dominate, with resolved package provenance, genuine terminal mappings and supported gestures. Estimated **6–10 h**, ~six minutes ideal media transfer at 100 Mbps. Do not add its 7,246 markers to an `answer` quota.

**Google AitW — the 494 GB figure is real, but it is not an all-or-nothing download.** The [official release](https://github.com/google-research/google-research/tree/master/android_in_the_wild) reports **715,142 episodes / 5,689,993 steps / 30,378 distinct prompts**, collected across eight devices and Android 10–13. The complete paginated GCS listing totals **493,592,603,661 bytes**. Subsets are General **13.62 GB**, GoogleApps **374.82 GB**, Install **35.96 GB**, Single **13.41 GB**, WebShopping **55.71 GB**, plus split metadata. [Recorded size audit](datasets_scratch/aitw_size_audit.json).

GZIP TFRecords carry encoded screenshots, dimensions, `current_activity`, episode ID, and actions. Important detail: stored touch/lift fields are **`yx_touch` / `yx_lift`**, i.e. y before x, even though descriptions may discuss x/y points. `DUAL_POINT` combines tap and swipe, and lacks the project's exact scroll semantics; text types into the focused field; back/home/Enter/status have direct equivalents. No factual answer labels. Actual full-release screenshot dimensions and AW-package prevalence were not enumerated in this audit; read the per-record dimensions and package-bearing activity before conversion. Collection predates AW; app overlap can still occur.

GoogleApps has very many trajectories but only a few hundred prompt templates. The AMEX paper's sampled reannotation found **93.43% agreement** on a random AitW subset and illustrates errors; that is not proof that 6.57% of all AitW rows are wrong. I did not locate an explicit dataset-specific licence grant in the original README, full paper or bucket listing; the arXiv page's CC BY label licenses the *paper*, and the monorepo Apache licence covers code. Commercial data/model clearance is therefore **unresolved**, not established by a third-party HF mirror.

**REJECT for this 60–100K-row v2**, because of redundant templates, filtering effort and unresolved data terms. A future General/Install-only experiment is feasible without 494 GB. Estimated **12–24 h**; full transfer ideal **11 h at 100 Mbps**. The cost is an opportunity-cost argument, not an assertion that AitW is unavailable.

**Other Google resources.** [UIBert RefExp](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/uibert) is a real CC BY 4.0, Rico-based grounding source; original train TFRecord is **40,934,636 bytes**, with dev/test about 1.28/1.54 MB. RefExp pairs one screen and a referring expression with a target-box index, which can supply filtered grounding; AppSim pairs two screens and is incompatible with this single-image input without redefining the task. Use the Atlas/original UIBert alternative already evaluated above, not an additional quota. Original split alignment should be established before using the Atlas conversion.

[Screen Annotation](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/screen_annotation) publishes train/validation/test CSVs of **16,993,478 / 2,904,772 / 5,323,569 bytes**, corresponding to **15,743 / 2,364 / 4,310 Rico screens**. The data licence is explicitly CC BY 4.0, with Rico media conditions. It serializes UI elements and attributes; emitting a whole screen annotation is not an allowed agent action. Deriving point queries would require source-box/type validation and app filtering, about **8–12 h**, without new images. **REJECT from v2** as a redundant and output-heavy auxiliary task; there is no demonstrated small-policy gain to justify that work.

[seq2act](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/seq2act) has public recreated PixelHelp TFRecords (ten files, about **0.075 GB**) and AndroidHowTo annotation pointers. The [PixelHelp README](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/seq2act/blob/master/data/pixel_help/README.md) specifies **187 multi-step instructions**: 88 general, 38 Gmail, 31 Chrome and 30 Photos tasks. Its documented features are UI-object text, geometry, hierarchy and action/target sequences; it does not document encoded screenshot features. The [authors' data-generation notes](https://github.com/google-research/google-research/tree/master/seq2act/data_generation) distinguish recreated releases from original experiments where copyright prevented direct release. AndroidHowTo reconstruction requires thousands of ~1–2 GB Common Crawl WARC files; it is not a ready screenshot-action corpus. **REJECT for v2**: this small evaluation resource is not a demonstrated screenshot-only training pool, and source-specific commercial terms/image availability are unresolved. No AW target app is identified by those four task categories, but no package-level payload audit was performed. Investigating a screenshot join would take roughly **4–8 h** before any usable-row commitment; expected incremental value is low for all three target evaluations/model sizes.

**Samsung MoIT/MoUI — available annotations, unresolved licence.** The real host is [`iitbsrib/MoUI`](https://github.com/iitbsrib/MoUI), not a guessed Samsung HF dataset name. The [paper](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1295.pdf) identifies Samsung Research Bangalore and IIT Bombay, describes ~150K instruction examples and a separate ~3K MoIQ benchmark. The public tree contains **49,900,992 bytes of train/val/test annotation JSONs** across grounding, icon/OCR/widget recognition, spatial reasoning, widget captioning and summaries. Screenshots are recovered from Rico, not shipped as a new corpus.

Full downloaded subsets contain **15,135 train find-icon rows**, **15,580 train widget-caption rows**, and **8,000 train screen-summary rows**. Their fields include `screen_id`, question, answer and sometimes `node_id`, hint and bbox. Find-icon answers are normalized-looking `[x1,y1,x2,y2]` strings; a caption sample's separate bbox is in pixels. A universal “all coordinates are already 0–1000” converter would be unsafe without task-specific checks. Point localization loses box supervision; some recognition/spatial tasks condition on explicit regions and cannot simply be presented as an unmodified screenshot with an underspecified question. QA can map to `answer` only when the text identifies the relevant region without privileged hints.

The 20/11/7 conservative AW-overlap counts above are already nonzero in those three portions; the other tasks were not fully scanned. The data are Rico-derived, not AC episodes. The paper/README reports useful 1B UI-QA results, but its synthetic instruction generation and auxiliary hints need visual QA, and those numbers do not establish AC/AW agent gains. **REJECT pending an explicit data licence**: the repository has no LICENSE and no data-licence paragraph; public accessibility does not establish commercial training/model redistribution rights. Estimated **6–12 h** if terms are supplied. Keep `MoIQ/moiq_benchmark.json` out of training.

**NVIDIA LocateAnything — provenance-preserving repackaging is still repackaging.** [The public release](https://huggingface.co/datasets/NVEagle/LocateAnything-Data) has machine-readable `datasets.jsonl`, `views.jsonl`, media-pool manifests and a [release policy](https://huggingface.co/datasets/NVEagle/LocateAnything-Data/blob/main/release-policy.json). Its OS-Atlas component has **1,904,625 records**, **1,857,250 media items**, about **678.65 GB of media tar files** and a **1.519 GB records file**, plus indices. Those are mixed-platform figures, not mobile-only counts. GroundCUA and ScaleCUA entries do not imply a new Android corpus.

Records target detection/grounding/pointing, not navigation histories or terminals. Filtering the repackaged OS-Atlas origin could in principle remove AW provenance and retain only mobile references, but that full filtering was not performed. No common resolution can be assumed; source dimensions are required. The README explicitly preserves original licences and requires source terms compliance; a NVIDIA model licence is not the data licence. **REJECT for this v2** because direct filtered Atlas/AMEX/Rico offers the same relevant signal with much less engineering and transfer. Estimated **8–16 h** just to build and check a reliable filtered reader; ~15 h ideal transfer for the OS-Atlas component at 100 Mbps. No independently established label-quality improvement over the upstream source was found.

**OpenMobile and OpenMobile-2 — direct AW provenance makes them inappropriate here.** [`cckevinn/OpenMobile-Data`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/cckevinn/OpenMobile-Data) has a **12,637,893,003-byte** raw-dump archive and four training JSONs totaling ~174 MB; the [project](https://njucckevin.github.io/openmobile/) reports roughly 2.8K tasks / 34K steps over 20 apps using AndroidWorld. [`OpenMobile-2/OpenMobile-Data-v2`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenMobile-2/OpenMobile-Data-v2) reports **11,643 trajectories / 118,582 steps / 59.2 GB**, divided into emulator 7,725/83,631, simulated-GUI 2,144/22,857 and hybrid 1,774/12,094. It also supplies 2,475 RL tasks. Small listings expose task names such as `OsmAndTrack`, `MarkorEditNote` and `ExpenseDeleteDuplicates`.

V2's documented input retains up to the last three screenshots, uses a 999×999 coordinate space, and mixes GUI/tool-call behavior. Dropping prior images or hybrid state is not lossless. Neither inspected card establishes a clear data licence. **REJECT both** for a strict AW-held-out evaluation; the simulator-only component would still need independent task-origin, licence and input-compatibility checks. Exact app counts and image resolutions across the full payload were not measured because the provenance rejection is already decisive. This is not a claim that their data cannot train a strong AW-specialized agent.

For a further cautionary example outside the requested company list, **Kwai's MobileForge** has a real [public exploration release](https://huggingface.co/datasets/lgy0404/mobileforge-exploration-trajectories): publisher **520 trajectories / 5,839 steps**, 3.70 GB raw plus 2.30 GB parsed assets, Apache card. Its package table contains **all 14 AW apps**. It is generated target-app exploration rather than human demonstrations; the full action/media payload was not inspected. **REJECT** for this held-out-app objective regardless of small download size. It must not be misattributed to Alibaba or treated as benchmark-independent data.

**Conversion requirements that determine whether the recommendation is viable.** None of these full trajectory corpora can honestly be promised as wholly lossless under the current action space. The viable subset can preserve next-action intent, while explicitly discarding unsupported cases:

1. **Use the actual screenshot frame.** Pixel points need the project's endpoint convention, approximately `round(1000*x/(width-1))`, and the analogous y formula; pre-normalized sources need their own documented convention. The 0–1000 integer grid itself quantizes points. Check for out-of-range coordinates rather than silently clamping corrupt labels. Uniform resizing preserves normalized position; cropping, letterboxing or rotation does not unless accounted for.
2. **Invert finger movement into revealed-content direction.** For ordinary page scrolling, a finger swipe upward usually means project `scroll down`. Horizontal finger-left usually reveals content to the right. Reject drags, drawing, slider movements, pinch/multitouch, diagonal gestures and region-specific scrolls that the fixed executor cannot reproduce. Even accepted cardinal swipes lose length; do not call this exact gesture reproduction.
3. **Preserve observation/action causality.** Build history from previous actions only, capped at the existing eight entries. Do not put future text, generated hindsight state, target bboxes, XML or accessibility content into model inputs. If a source action cannot be represented, reject the dependent remainder or whole episode; do not silently splice across an omitted action. Sampling training rows need not erase the real earlier history used to construct each row.
4. **Keep actions separate.** A focused-field `TYPE` is not click-plus-type or automatic Enter. Do not split a combined focus-and-write label into two decisions while reusing the same screenshot for both: the intermediate observation is missing. Map genuine completion/infeasibility labels to status, not `answer`; map factual screen answers to `answer`. Do not append a terminal action to the final pre-action image merely because the recording ends.
5. **Treat static grounding as an auxiliary task.** A target rectangle is not evidence that clicking it is correct. Require an interactive target and a query whose semantics are satisfied by the click. Keep one expression per image/target in the sampling pool and reuse original screenshot IDs to prevent duplicate weighting across repackagings.
6. **Validate the actual small-model view.** The local `MAX_PIXELS = 880*880` and 32-pixel factor are not a universal hard 720-token limit; the code comment itself says approximately 756. Resize/tokenize with each model's processor and enforce the desired budget explicitly before selecting readable samples. Check tiny text and small targets at that rendered size, especially tablets and 1440-wide Chinese screens. No code was changed by this audit.

For implementation QA, save source repo revision, original file hash, source episode/screen ID, source split, package/app evidence, raw action and conversion rule per selected row. Reject unknown AC source IDs; require every AC-derived row to be in official train and outside the existing AW filter. For independently collected sources, source/package provenance and a later exact-image/perceptual-hash check against frozen test media are complementary. AW app-family exclusion is deliberately stricter than merely removing exact benchmark tasks. Keep dev/test media out of training, and do not tune this mix on the frozen AC test or the 116 AW outcomes.

**Recommended v2 allocation.** This is an **80K total-row replacement mix**, not 80K additions to the existing ~99K. Local `results/data/dataset_verification.json` records a historical VM build of 99,216 rows: 74,595 high-level decisions, 13,573 terminals and 11,048 duplicated low-level rows over 13,573 episodes. That file is historical evidence, not a fresh enumeration of a live training store. Rebuild the selection manifest from current permitted train records before running.

| Source / purpose | Target rows | Proportion | Selection rule |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| AndroidControl original train, high-level next actions | 50,000 | 62.5% | Existing AW exclusion; balance apps/goals/actions; no step hint for the main policy task |
| AndroidControl original train, terminal observations | 6,000 | 7.5% | Genuine post-task observations; avoid overwhelming next-action learning with completion |
| GUI-Odyssey new version | 10,000 | 12.5% | Supported phone-screen decisions and causal histories; reserve at most ~600 rows for validated terminals |
| AMEX trajectories | 8,000 | 10.0% | Exclude flagged/bad episodes; target up to 500 valid Enter rows and ~500 reviewed terminal rows within this allocation |
| ScreenQA train | 6,000 | 7.5% | 5,000 short answers + 1,000 concise full answers on **different questions**; AW-app/image/readability filtering |
| **Total** | **80,000** | **100%** | Unique source decisions/questions; no mirrored-source double counting |

These are acquisition/sampling targets, not claims that those exact counts have already survived every visual filter. If fewer rare-action rows survive, use other supported AMEX decisions; never duplicate questionable labels to hit a quota. Keep examples from the same episode/image together when defining any new-source development holdout. Build full causal histories first, then select rows. The quota includes terminal examples, so do not add another terminal copy afterward.

For a **450M grounding ablation**, replace 2–4K of the 50K AC high-level rows with original permitted AC low-level click/long-press rows, or one licensed and filtered UIBert/Widget/Aria source. Keep the total 80K. This is a hypothesis worth testing on validation, not an assumption that a base model's ScreenSpot result makes grounding irrelevant. Do not introduce long synthetic reasoning: use short action intents, normally a sentence, without unsupported hindsight.

For **lower acquisition cost**, replace the 8K AMEX block with filtered GUIAct smartphone decisions, retaining its Enter cases as `keyboard_enter`. Full fresh image acquisition falls from roughly **193 GB** for Odyssey + AMEX + Rico to roughly **103 GB** for Odyssey + GUIAct + Rico, plus annotations. Resolving GUIAct package provenance is a condition of that cheaper variant. Conversely, if cross-app histories cannot be represented faithfully, replace part of Odyssey with more independently selected AMEX decisions rather than accepting hidden-state shortcuts.

The default full-source acquisition is about **4.3 h at an ideal 100 Mbps** or **26 minutes at 1 Gbps**, before throttling, extraction and checks. Real elapsed time can be much longer. These are transfer estimates, not purchased cloud egress quotes; provider charges depend on the chosen host. Budget disk for both archives and extracted images, and measure extraction expansion rather than assuming compressed size equals disk usage. Reusing existing AC images avoids another ~50 GB. A practical engineering allowance for the default ingestion, shared filters and spot QA is **roughly 35–55 engineer-hours**.

At the supplied **one training sample/second** for 2B, one pass costs **16.7 h for 60K**, **22.2 h for 80K**, or **27.8 h for 100K** before evaluation/checkpoint overhead. Two passes double that; training all four model variants requires separate runs. Actual throughput changes with model processor, sequence length and answer length, so 450M speed should be measured, not assumed. An 80K mixed run and an equal-budget AC-only control are more informative than four unpaired source additions. Commercial release remains conditional on resolving the AC terms and documenting the chosen source/media rights; the row mix does not fix a licence conflict.

**What was wrong or overstated in the recommendation.**

- **“These all convert losslessly” is wrong.** Raw gestures, combined writes, bbox tasks, multi-image histories and terminal markers have distinct semantics. The current fixed action format cannot preserve every field or interaction.
- **“Exclude `aw_mobile.json` and Atlas is AW-clean” is wrong.** Its AW media archive must also be excluded, and full Rico joins still find VLC/OsmAnd references in UIBert, Widget Captioning and RICOSCA.
- **GUI-Actor should not be rejected for a demonstrated AC-test leak.** Its inspected file has zero such overlap. Its problems here are duplicate existing train data, limited action coverage and unresolved data terms. The paper's rounded count is not the current file's 39,701 rows.
- **Aria mobile is not a million new mobile screens or trajectories.** It expands AMEX labels and needs AMEX media. Adding Aria, AMEX and Atlas-AMEX without deduplication exaggerates diversity.
- **AMEX's element headline is not the count of populated functionality descriptions or demonstrated actions.** The release has 38,709 trajectory steps and the author confirms a smaller functionality-description subset.
- **“There are no public answer examples” is wrong.** ScreenQA has real answer labels. GUIAct's `answer` field is different: it contains 7,236 complete and 10 impossible markers, not factual answers. That does not make GUIAct useless for trajectory training.
- **All Widget Captioning rows are not usable screenshot examples.** The 12,960 `nr` rows have no public screenshots. ScreenQA short/full answers and Screen2Words' five summaries also must not be counted as independent screens.
- **A permissive code licence does not clear all data or resulting model rights.** AndroidControl has an explicit primary-source conflict; GUI-Actor and MoIT lack an identified standalone data grant; Screen2Words lacks an identified dataset licence; Rico has its own media terms.
- **The search should include actual 2026 releases.** CMGUI and GUI-Libra are real candidates. Samsung's MoIT is downloadable, although not commercially cleared by an identified licence. UI-S1 is downloadable but unsafe for this frozen-test setup without restored provenance. Model-only UI-TARS/Ferret releases must not be advertised as downloadable full training corpora.
- **Large downloads are a practical cost, not proof of nonviability.** Odyssey and AMEX merit small sampled row quotas despite large monolithic media; AitW has smaller subsets; GUIAct is a much cheaper source of Enter/trajectory supervision. The decisive selection criteria are provenance, semantic fit, incremental coverage and label quality within the fixed row budget.

The supporting downloaded source files, complete small-file audits and byte ledger remain in [datasets_scratch](datasets_scratch/). Findings above distinguish publisher claims, exact annotation counts, bounded samples, unresolved licences and estimated benefits so that acquisition can proceed without turning an assumption into a benchmark-cleanliness or deployment claim.
# Training data v2 — what is viable (Codex gpt-6-astra review, web-searched, 2026-11-16)

Full report with citations: `results/codex/dataset_viability.md` (70 KB). Small audited files: `results/codex/datasets_scratch/`.

## Verdicts

| Dataset | Verdict | Why |
|---|---|---|
| GUI-Odyssey (new `hflqf88888/GUIOdyssey`, 8,334 eps / 127,893 steps, CC BY 4.0, 92.6 GB) | **USE-WITH-FILTER** | cross-app diversity; filter unsupported gestures, AW-app labels, orientation issues |
| AMEX trajectories (3,046 eps / 38,709 steps, CC BY 4.0, 93.5 GB) | **USE-WITH-FILTER** | new apps, real Enter + terminal cases; drop flagged/inconsistent episodes |
| ScreenQA (Google, Rico; 68,951 train Qs, CC BY annotations) | **USE-WITH-FILTER** | the only real source of `answer` supervision (my claim "no public answer data" was wrong) |
| GUIAct smartphone (7,246 eps / 65,627 steps) | cheaper alternative to AMEX | Enter supervision; its `answer` field is only "task complete" markers |
| OS-Atlas mobile | USE-WITH-FILTER, small | must drop `aw_mobile.json` **and** its AW media archive; Rico joins still hit VLC/OsmAnd |
| Aria-UI mobile | USE-WITH-FILTER, small | expands AMEX labels (not 1M new screens) — dedupe vs AMEX |
| Widget Captioning | USE-WITH-FILTER, optional | only `rc` rows have public screenshots |
| GUI-Actor AC boxes (Microsoft) | REJECT for v2 | duplicates our AC train, no standalone data licence (no test leak found) |
| Screen2Words | REJECT | no explicit dataset licence located |
| Alibaba CMGUI | default 0 | Chinese-heavy; only if Chinese deployment matters |
| Alibaba UI-S1, Samsung MoIT, NVIDIA LocateAnything, AITW full | REJECT for v2 | lost provenance / no data licence / repackaging / 494 GB |
| OpenMobile / OpenMobile-2 | REJECT | built on AndroidWorld environment/tasks (contaminates AW) |
| UI-TARS (ByteDance), Ferret-UI (Apple), GUI-Owl (Alibaba), AppAgent (Tencent) | not available | model/code only, no full public training corpora |

## Recommended v2 mix (80K rows, replacement not addition)

| Source | Rows | % |
|---|---:|---:|
| AndroidControl train, high-level next actions | 50,000 | 62.5 |
| AndroidControl terminal observations | 6,000 | 7.5 |
| GUI-Odyssey (new) | 10,000 | 12.5 |
| AMEX trajectories (≤500 Enter, ~500 reviewed terminals) | 8,000 | 10.0 |
| ScreenQA (5k short + 1k full answers, different questions) | 6,000 | 7.5 |

Run it against an **equal-budget AndroidControl-only control**, so the gain is attributable to data diversity. On an L4,
80K rows ≈ 22 h for a 2B model (1 row/s), so start with one model (LFM3-VL-2B). Image acquisition is ~193 GB
(Odyssey + AMEX + Rico); the GUIAct variant is ~103 GB.

Corrections Codex made to my original recommendation: conversion is **not lossless** (gestures, multi-image histories,
terminal markers differ); OS-Atlas needs more than dropping `aw_mobile.json`; ScreenQA **is** a public answer source;
code licences do not clear data rights.
# v2 SFT data audit — 2026-11-17

**Verdict: NO-GO for training the published v2 revision.** There is confirmed train/dev screen leakage, demonstrably incompatible action supervision, and premature completion labels. Valid JSON and matching builder statistics do not clear this mix.

Audited dataset: `experimentalmachines/AndroidAgent-SFT-v2` at **`8e0e63a48f180aa60c103d2f492d6364355cb306`**. Downloaded exactly **three v2 train shards, 00000/00003/00004**, selected with seed `20261117`, and all three dev shards: **15,000 train + 1,999 dev = 16,999 rows**. Dataset files/card/stats totaled **1,626,522,302 bytes**. The remaining 13 train shards were not downloaded or inspected. Findings are confirmed instances or explicitly bounded sample results, not a certification of all 79,991 advertised train rows.

I decoded and hashed every downloaded image, ran the actual strict action parser and resize rule, joined primary-source annotations, inspected **40 random train rows per new source**, and performed 27 additional targeted review instances, some overlapping the random sample, plus five AC screens for app-identity review. Red crosshairs use `agent_format.norm_to_px`; images were not generated or altered except for analytical overlays. No GPU training or live device replay was performed.

| ID | Severity | Finding and evidence | Exact fix before training |
|---|---|---|---|
| F1 | **blocking** | **Five train/dev Odyssey pairs have identical JPEG bytes and decoded pixels**, despite different episode IDs. Two are PIN-entry screens and three are launchers; several also have closely related goals. All pairs are listed below. | Compute canonical screenshot hashes across **all** candidate train/dev rows before publishing. Remove train rows with dev hashes, or assign connected episode/screen groups to one split. Include reviewed near-duplicate clusters and task-template siblings in the split audit. Assert zero train/dev image-hash intersection on the rebuilt full dataset. |
| F2 | **major** | **The shared type-only contract differs from the AndroidWorld executor.** The actual executor function, run with recording ADB stubs, executes `type_text("Art"), Enter, Enter` for `input_text("Art")` followed by `keyboard_enter`. **99/100** inspected AMEX Enter rows immediately follow raw `TYPE` steps. v1 has no `keyboard_enter` targets; v2 introduces this explicit second-submit pattern. [Probe](data_v2_cache/executor_probe.json), [executor](../../android_world/android_world/env/actuation.py), [protocol](../../docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md). | Resolve the execution contract explicitly. To retain the shared format, implement a type-only agent adapter and compare **both v1 and v2** with that adapter; keep standard-protocol AW scores separately labeled. If standard AW auto-Enter must remain, retain only trajectories whose observations/actions remain valid under it. Do not blindly delete Enter rows or fabricate intermediate screenshots. This is a pre-existing v1 mismatch that the new Enter allocation makes more consequential. |
| F3 | **major** | **Cardinal direction does not establish executable scroll semantics.** AMEX `87dde1a8dfb3407a980f2fbc9e4572ff/12` scrolls a narrow time dropdown. Raw finger path is `(946,725)→(967,1547)` on 1440×3040. The actual AW `scroll up` path is `(720,1520)→(720,3040)`, outside the dropdown. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/visual/amex_21_scroll_paths.png). Builder accepts it at `data/build_v2_mix.py:309`. | Reject region-specific scrolls, drags, and other gestures that the fixed center executor cannot reproduce, and truncate dependent history at that step. Alternatively extend and test a shared region-aware action contract in builder, trainer and executor before including them. Checking only angle and distance is insufficient. |
| F4 | **major** | **AMEX key-like text is treated as literal typing.** Five downloaded train targets are `input_text("esc")`; `53cc23e9f1c0492c900cf960c4a4a9a7/13` is a non-editable article screen. Independent reconstruction selects **17 `esc` targets and one newline-only target** before image filtering. Two inspected histories also contain newline-only typing. [Article example](data_v2_cache/visual/extra_amex_05.png), [selection](data_v2_cache/amex_selected_before_images.jsonl). | Validate `TYPE` payload semantics. Quarantine Escape-like actions and their dependent suffixes unless source execution establishes a supported equivalent. Map genuine Enter events to `keyboard_enter` only after resolving F2; preserve actual multiline text. Do not train literal `esc`, or automatically assume Escape equals Back. |
| F5 | **major** | **Source terminal markers are not reliable goal-completion evidence.** Odyssey `5965127172744421/14` and `4704062497509653/11` say complete while Play Store still says **Pending**. `9545981158176326/8` maps `INCOMPLETE` to infeasible merely because a Facebook search did not find the desired information. [Terminal sheet](data_v2_cache/visual/terminals_sheet_1.jpg), [incomplete example](data_v2_cache/visual/odyssey_14.png), raw reasons in [provenance](data_v2_cache/provenance.json). | Require reviewed post-goal observations for complete labels; discard pending/loading endpoints when no later observation exists. Distinguish abandoned/unsuccessful attempts from genuine infeasibility. Quarantine unreviewed `INCOMPLETE`/`TASK_IMPOSSIBLE` labels rather than filling terminal quotas with them. |
| F6 | **major** | **Some information-seeking trajectories end silently.** AMEX `14eeb4cbf4674199bbb878225d0773f4/13` asks for the latest show time; the screenshot shows times, but supervision is `status complete`. Eight sampled train complete rows have question-mark goals and need review; this heuristic is not itself an error count. [Example](data_v2_cache/visual/amex_20.png). AW stores the answer text and ends on `answer` in `eval/androidworld/lfm_agent.py:178`. | Review terminal goals requiring a response. Emit a screenshot/history-supported `answer` where the answer is verified; otherwise drop that terminal. Keep factual answers distinct from task-status acknowledgments across every source, not just ScreenQA. |
| F7 | **major** | **Additional source-label contradictions survive the filters.** Odyssey `1252281995307488/0` points outside the stated recents card. AMEX `1cb9048b34114235b0c8031feb3612f0/10` selects `mark.tylor@gmail.com` instead of required `mark.taylor@gmail.com`; `aca93ad65ded4e1598871da20bc5b479/5` types “morning mediation” instead of “Morning Meditation.” ScreenQA `rico10893/10963` omits visible “Basic Concepts.” [Row-level judgments](data_v2_cache/visual_review.md). | Add goal/entity consistency and visual target checks. Correct labels only when the intended target/answer is supported by the observation. For trajectory errors, quarantine the erroneous step and dependent suffix; changing an earlier typed string without re-recording later screenshots would invent a trajectory. Correct/drop the static QA independently. |
| F8 | **major** | **The blanket AW-app exclusion claim is too broad, and the planned 14-app claim remains partly unverified.** Chrome is present by exact package in AMEX and ScreenQA; Settings/Clock/Contacts appear in AC, and Settings/Chrome/Clock are visible in Odyssey. For the intended 14 third-party app families, no exact package hit was found in inspected AMEX/ScreenQA rows, but AC lacks package provenance, five AC rows refer to `Calendar Pro`, and **106 sampled train + 60 dev Odyssey rows** have unresolved `Calendar` app labels. | State a frozen, explicit exclusion policy: the 14 held-out app families versus shared built-in apps. Retain a per-step package/app provenance manifest. Resolve Calendar/Calendar Pro and other ambiguous aliases to packages or conservatively exclude their episodes, as the earlier viability review required for Calendar. Re-audit AC with source package evidence. Do not describe the present dataset as excluding every AW app. |
| F9 | **minor** | **The advertised AMEX three-row episode cap is bypassed by quota selection.** Running the real sampler on a fixture returns six rows from one episode with cap three. Reconstructing all AMEX candidates yields **19 selected episodes above three, maximum seven** before image filtering. All 1,469 inspected AMEX train keys match that reconstruction; the three-shard sample alone has no observed cap violation. [Reproduction](data_v2_cache/sampler_repro.json), [full reconstruction](data_v2_cache/provenance.json). | Apply uniqueness, total-row limit and episode cap during **every quota pass**, then during filler selection. Retain the existing exclusion of quota categories from the filler. A corrected simulation still obtains 8,000 rows and all four quotas, with maximum three per episode. [Simulation](data_v2_cache/sampler_fixed_simulation.json). |
| F10 | **minor** | **AMEX reasoning is largely an action template, not an intent.** All **801/801 sampled AMEX train clicks** say “Tap the next element needed for the task.” This is 54.5% of sampled AMEX rows. The text adds no target/state information to an intent-in-`<think>` model or its eight-step history. Harm to model accuracy is a risk inference, not a measured training result. | **Blank templated nonterminal AMEX `reasoning_content` and corresponding history intent strings**, preserving action JSON and `history_text` formatting. Retain only validated terminal intents or genuinely reviewed, screenshot-grounded target intents. Do not generate speculative reasoning merely to fill the field. Verify empty-think rendering for the actual training template. |
| F11 | **minor** | **Duplicates exist, but most are not identical training examples.** Six excess train rows share image+action; only **one** also has identical messages: AC episodes `8110/0` and `14189/0`. ScreenQA `rico40184/38672` and `/38675` ask different questions with the same answer, “disabled.” | Deduplicate exact image+messages examples. Separately cap repeated screen/action weighting and inspect repeated no-change decisions. Do not discard valid different QA questions solely because image+answer matches. |
| F12 | **minor** | **Coordinate-frame provenance is incomplete.** AMEX `ba060ad1f8c04af1afad922de5f78906/9` declares a 3120×1440 device but has a 1440×3120 source image. Its normalized point still plausibly hits Favorite; this is **not a demonstrated missed click**. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/visual/amex_rotation.png). | Record the actual coordinate-frame transform and verify orientation mismatches against targets. Quarantine unresolved cases; do not blindly transpose dimensions, which would move this plausible point away from Favorite. Save raw action, screenshot ID/hash, dimensions and conversion rule per selected row. |

