results/codex/codex_eval_pass1.md:84:**Minor — scroll verification overclaim.** No inversion is performed (`:92–93`), and AW's `scroll down` physically moves toward the top, revealing content below (`android_world/android_world/env/actuation.py:138–165`). The phase-correlation artifact has 154 vertical agreements and 79 disagreements among 233 informative examples, with 810 additional unclear vertical cases. Horizontal evidence is only 4 agreements / 6 disagreements, with 158 unclear. This supports a majority vertical convention, not a clean empirical proof for all directions; I found no evidence requiring a global inversion. The older row-mean check is still noisier. **Fix:** retain the check script and representative manually inspected transition pairs, verify all four directions, and correct the artifact path in `docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md:33` to `results/data/scroll_convention_check.json`.
results/codex/codex_eval_pass1.md:96:The active L4 job requests 1,250 optimizer steps × effective batch 32 = about 40,000 rows, **0.403 epochs** of the reported 99,216-row mixture (`scripts/jobs/lfm3_train_l4.json:11–16`). Rows include terminal targets and low-mode copies, so this is not 40,000 independent trajectories. A full pass is a defensible initial format/behavior-adaptation budget for an already pretrained 2B VLM; it is not evidence that navigation has saturated.
results/codex/codex_eval_pass1.md:104:The builder puts JSON in assistant `content` and the instruction in `reasoning_content` (`data/build_androidcontrol.py:231–233`). `train/sft_vlm.py:121–124` enables assistant-only loss and disables truncation. This is not merely an ignored configuration flag in the pinned libraries: [TRL 1.16.0's vision collator](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/sft_trainer.py#L385) prepares image-first messages, requests `return_assistant_tokens_mask`, and masks non-assistant labels to −100 at lines 412–415. Its multimodal helper preserves `reasoning_content`.
results/codex/codex_eval_pass1.md:116:The official AndroidControl [action definition](https://github.com/google-research/google-research/blob/master/android_control/README.md#action-space) says `input_text` does **not** press Enter. AW's executor does (`android_world/android_world/env/actuation.py:104–105`), and `lfm_agent.py:124–131` uses that executor directly. The shared system prompt only says it types into the focused field (`train/agent_format.py:27`). This is a verified transition mismatch even though `docs/DATASETS_AND_SPLITS.md:76–77` acknowledges AW's behavior. A text-entry training example can therefore teach a different resulting screen than the online action produces.
results/codex/codex_eval_pass1.md:147:`warmup_steps=0.03` is **not a bug in the pinned Transformers version**. [Transformers 5.20.2](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/v5.20.2/src/transformers/training_args.py#L2118) interprets values below 1 as a fraction, yielding 38 warmup steps for 1,250 updates. The observed LRs agree with that schedule. A larger initial unclipped gradient norm also does not establish instability; `max_grad_norm=1.0` is configured.
results/codex/codex_eval_pass1.md:270:| Next SFT candidate | **Qwen3.5-2B** | The [official model card](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.5-2B) verifies a vision encoder plus 2B-language-backbone hybrid and explicitly positions the small model for task-specific tuning. The [llama.cpp project](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/README.md) demonstrates Qwen3.5-family GGUF support, but I did not verify a publisher-owned 2B GGUF artifact or this exact model on a phone. Validate conversion/runtime parity before committing a full training budget. |
