results/codex/codex_data_v2_audit.md:89:Source shares are **69.95% AC / 12.94% Odyssey / 9.79% AMEX / 7.31% ScreenQA**. Overall status share is **1,366/15,000 = 9.11%**; answer share is **7.31%**. Since the AW harness also terminates after `answer`, **16.42%** of sampled labels end an episode under that harness. v1's same-revision cached metadata contains 99,216 rows, **13,573 status rows (13.68%)**, and no answer or keyboard_enter targets. These numbers measure label allocation, not terminal correctness.
results/codex/codex_data_v2_audit.md:93:**Quotas and caps.** The quota-as-ceiling rule works for the current disjoint predicates: filler skips every quota category. Reconstructing AMEX's entire small annotation archive yields **34,450 train candidates** and, before image validation, 8,000 selected rows: 500 Enter, 500 complete, 150 infeasible, 1,000 type, 4,424 click, 1,406 scroll, 18 back, 2 home. This matches the builder's claimed 7,998 materialized rows after its reported removal of one click and one infeasible row; those two image removals were not independently checked against raw media. Every inspected train AMEX key belongs to the reconstructed selection. The cap defect is in the quota passes, not the filler; fixing it can preserve the allocation without adding duplicate rows. ScreenQA's observed train per-screen cap and Odyssey's observed train per-episode cap hold in this sample. AC has no episode cap and uses uniform row sampling rather than the plan's app/goal/action balancing.
results/codex/codex_data_v2_audit.md:95:**Implications for the 2B model.** The promising additions are real screen answers, diverse apps, and explicit actions absent from v1. The most concrete regression risks are premature stopping, silent responses to information requests, typing special-key names, learning scrolls the executor cannot perform, and double submission after typing. Generic AMEX thoughts also weaken the useful target/state descriptions that v1 puts into history; blanking them avoids supervising meaningless prose but does not magically supply missing state. Odyssey histories average **5.30 entries / 688 user-text characters**, versus AC **3.29 / 436**; verify cross-app memory sufficiency and actual token throughput rather than assuming a row always costs the same GPU time. No accuracy improvement or regression was measured here. After repair, use the plan's equal-budget AC-only control on clean development splits, and keep frozen AC TEST/AW outcomes out of mix tuning.
